{
  "ocpnName": "Airport Ground Handling (Extended)",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "Airport Ground Handling (Extended)",
    "description": "A fictitious Central-European hub — Danube International (DNB/LHDN) — sized like Budapest Ferenc Liszt: two runways, two piers of contact stands either side of the Schengen border, a remote apron, and roughly 175 arrivals a day.\n\nThe process follows the A-CDM milestone approach (M1 flight plan filed through M16 take-off). Each turnaround splits into five concurrent threads — cabin, hold, fuel, cabin service and departure planning — that rejoin at ARDT, so the critical path emerges from resource contention rather than being fixed in the model.\n\nGround vehicles carry their apron position, so driving a fuel tanker, catering truck, ambulift or tug from its last stand to the next one costs real time; tankers run their load down and return to the depot to reload, while contact and code-E stands are fuelled from hydrant pits at a much higher flow rate. Passengers with reduced mobility (~1.1% of passengers) are individual objects, assisted by an agent at an airbridge or by an ambulift at a remote stand.\n\nAircraft are a based fleet of 141 airframes, each with a fixed registration, type and operator. A flight plan takes an airframe out of the pool and it returns after flying its outbound sector, so an aircraft is one long-lived object flying rotation after rotation rather than a fresh object per arrival.",
    "authors": [
      "István Koren"
    ],
    "version": "1.0",
    "license": "MIT",
    "created": "2026-08-11T22:42:27.446Z"
  },
